<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nair</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nairs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nair are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, who formed the warrior and landholding class of the region's kingdoms, serving as soldiers, administrators and local chiefs. They were known for their martial training in kalaripayattu and for matrilineal inheritance, in which property and family descended through women. Their traditional role declined under British rule, and matrilineal inheritance was abolished by law in the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nair.jpg" alt="Nair" width="275" height="360" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Thomas (1749-1840) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daniel_Thomas-A_Nair.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Nair, also called Nayar, are not one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> but, in the words of the anthropologist Kathleen Gough, a named category of castes, with many subdivisions whose customs differed between the north and the south of what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>. The origin of the name is disputed: some derive it from nayaka, an honorific meaning leader of the people, others from the community's link with the Naga cult of serpent worship. The anthropologist Christopher Fuller thinks the Nareae named by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> in his Natural History of 77 CE were probably the Nairs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">Copper</a>-plate inscriptions of about the 7th to 9th centuries, recording grants of land and rights to Jewish and Christian traders, name Nair chiefs and soldiers of Eranad, Valluvanad, Venad and Palghat as witnesses, which suggests that by then they were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> chieftains of the Perumals. Before British rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> was divided into about ten states under rajahs, subdivided into nadus governed by hereditary Nair chiefs, the naduvazhi, who kept order and could call on the military service of all Nairs below them, usually a permanent force of 500 to 1,000 men.</p><p>Their dominance first weakened after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marthanda-varma">Marthanda Varma</a> became Raja of Venad in 1729. He curbed the Nair chiefs, brought Tamil Brahmins into his administration and reorganised the Nair army in the European style as a standing force, which fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-colachel">Battle of Colachel</a> in 1741. After hostilities between the Nairs and the British in 1809 the British limited their place in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a>, and after independence the Nair Brigade of the Travancore State Force became part of the 9th Battalion of the Madras Regiment.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Nairs lived in tharavads, joint families of the descendants of one ancestress, often of 50 to 80 members and sometimes as many as 200. Only the women lived in the main house, and authority lay with the karnavan, the eldest man, who controlled the common property and arranged marriages: the head of the family was the mother's brother, not the mother. Girls went through the pre-puberty rite of thalikettu kalyanam and later the sambandham, after which a woman might take one or more partners. None of these rituals survives in any significant way.</p><p>Most Nairs were literate in Malayalam, as many served as scribes and bailiffs of the royal courts, and from the 16th century they contributed increasingly to literature and drama. Their chief deity is Bhagavati, goddess of war and fertility, and serpent deities were kept in groves on the family land.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nair” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…than in much of India until the mid-1800s, the government turning the caste order into a religious institution; <b>Nairs</b> and Tamil Brahmins alone dominated the bureaucracy into the early 20th century, and women of the backward castes were forbidden to wear an upper cloth, the cause of the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/travancore">Travancore · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Power was shared within a large matrilineal royal family whose branches held separate seats, and <b>Nair</b> warriors formed the backbone of society.</blockquote><a href="/state/kolathunadu">Kolathunadu</a></li></ul></section>
